    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nExecuting `:lua =vim.ui.open(\"https://neovim.io/\")` does not open the URL. I've pasted an example return value,\n\n```lua\n{\n  _state = {\n    cmd = <1>{ \"cmd.exe\", \"/c\", \"start\", \"\", \"https://neovim.io\" },\n    done = false,\n    handle = <userdata 1>,\n    pid = 17156,\n    stderr = <userdata 2>,\n    stderr_data = {},\n    stdout = <userdata 3>,\n    stdout_data = {}\n  },\n  cmd = <table 1>,\n  pid = 17156,\n  <metatable> = {\n    __index = {\n      _timeout = <function 1>,\n      is_closing = <function 2>,\n      kill = <function 3>,\n      wait = <function 4>,\n      write = <function 5>\n    }\n  }\n}\nnil\n```\n\nI can't seem to find a process for it with process explorer so I'm not sure what's happening. I can confirm that it works on the current stable version 0.12.4.\n\nRelated https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/39591\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nExecute `:lua =vim.ui.open(\"https://neovim.io/\")`\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nURL to open\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-1049+gad3720b882 Build type: RelWithDebInfo LuaJIT 2.1.1784360928\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nN/A\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nWindows 11\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nWindows Terminal\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nN/A\n\n### Installation\n\nbob",
